Coordinate scope correctness checks
===================================

Four modifiers share optional ACTION END support. The input stack is per kernel
and strict across modifier types. Unmatched tails retain legacy behavior.
Only a matched opener saves coordinates and the shared Pbc box. END swaps that
entry state back; backward propagation temporarily swaps into the interior frame
without recalculating CVs or overwriting virtual-atom Jacobians. At END.apply,
outside atomic forces and virial are saved and cleared. The opener applies its
existing derivative map only to interior contributions, then merges the bypassed
outside contributions and restores the outer frame again.

The serial fixture checks:
- all three fit methods, RESET_CELL, WHOLEMOLECULES and WRAPAROUND;
- restoration versus an unmodified run, and forces/full virial versus the sum
  of independently biased inside-only and outside-only runs;
- atomic and all nine affine-strain central differences, on image-stable inputs;
- same-type and mixed nesting, and biases after END on earlier/interior CVs;
- changing coordinates/cells across reused steps and nonzero incoming MD forces;
- earlier COM, interior COM and nonlinear GHOST, and deferred scalar outputs;
- exclusion of expired virtual atoms from later modifier force/coordinate maps;
- invalid nesting/options, labels/numeric/group/@allatoms references to expired
  atoms, INCLUDE, incremental parsing, two simultaneous kernels and clear;
- inherited stride, skipped steps, and dependency activation off the pilot stride.

Virtual atom numbers stay reserved after scope closure. An atom-reference
selection including an expired virtual atom fails explicitly, even if indirect
through GROUP or @allatoms. @mdatoms still selects only the host atoms. A CV
computed inside, or scalar outputs from an interior virtual atom, can be used
outside: these scalar dependencies do not change its definition frame.

The adjacent rt-make-coordinate-scopes-mpi fixture compares serial with native
MPI for sparse atom requests (four of six MD atoms), scattered and changing
ownership, and an empty rank on each side. Both host atomic forces and the sum
of host cell-force tensors must agree with the serial oracle. Use matching
Open MPI compiler/launcher wrappers; e.g. set PLUMED_MPIRUN to
'/opt/local/bin/mpiexec-openmpi-mp --host localhost:2 --bind-to none'.

rt-coordinate-scope-launch checks the existing launch scheduler. Each coordinate
frame interval contains two independent actions that must launch before either
is consumed. No launch crosses a scope opener or END, including the first step.
The fixture retains a deliberately late-discovered coordinate mutation to test
cancellation. Numerical reference files are independently generated with
PLUMED_LAUNCH=no; launch-enabled outputs match byte-for-byte locally.

Future coordinate modifiers, including PROJECT_COORDINATES once merged, should
call registerCoordinateScopeKeywords(keys) in registerKeywords and return early
from their constructor when initializeCoordinateScope() returns true. The shared
forward/backward hooks handle END; retain the action's own calculate/apply maps.
The current registration interface requires an ActionPilot and inherits its
schedule at END. Do not add process-global scope stacks or disable launch.
